The relevant function is
JavaScript:
Game_CharacterBase.prototype.distancePerFrame = function() {
    return Math.pow(2, this.realMoveSpeed()) / 256;
};
So, speed of 3.6921 would be
(2^3.6921)/256
, or about
0.05
tiles per frame.

I like using this to get the amount of frames it takes for a character to move 1 tile which you can use as a reference for wait frames (movement speed of 4 would take 16 frames per tile):
JavaScript:
const num = 2 ** (8 - $gameMap.event(1).moveSpeed());
this.wait(num);
As stated above, frequency will affect your timing as well. A setting of 5 (highest) would return 0, or, no stop count before executing next movement code:
JavaScript:
Game_Event.prototype.stopCountThreshold = function() {
    return 30 * (5 - this.moveFrequency());
};
